# Comments & DESNOTEs

Do not add code comments that simply restate the behavior of the code. If code
is sufficiently complicated that the functionality is not clear, it should be
refactored to be less complex or broken down in to more clear problems.

When the code does things that are strange, counter-intuitive, or relate to some
specific business usecase or library requirement, etc;, It should have a
DESNOTE. DESNOTEs should be written as `// DESNOTE(<author name>, <date>): ...
explanation`

Use the real author name and date — never placeholders. Adapt the comment token
to the language (e.g. `# DESNOTE(...)` in Python).

DESNOTEs should provide links to relevant information when possible (vendor docs,
specs, issue trackers, related discussions) so the reasoning can be verified.

```rust
// DESNOTE(jbarber, 2026-06-19): The vendor SDK returns 200 with an empty body
// on rate-limit instead of 429, so we treat an empty body here as a retry.
// See https://vendor.example.com/docs/rate-limiting
```
